  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gabay (Guide): Strengthening Inclusive Education for Blind/Deaf Children</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Philippines):  .USAID, through the Gabay (Guide) Strengthening Inclusive Education activity, improves access of deaf, blind, or deafblind children to quality education. USAID increases these children&apos;s access to schools, builds teacher capacity, and improves reading outcomes of children who are deaf and blind from Kindergarten to Grade 3. USAID also supports innovations and improvement of disability-inclusive education policy and programs. ..USAID operates in six school divisions: Batangas City, Sorsogon City, Maasin City, Batangas Province, Sorsogon Province, and Southern Leyte Province. In the first three years of implementation, USAID tripled the enrollment of deaf and blind students and started piloting a program for deafblind/blind students with other disabilities. USAID trained nearly 800 teachers and administrators in addressing educational needs of children with sensory disabilities. USAID produced and distributed over 5,000 learning materials in Braille and Filipino Sign Language, including a Filipino Sign Language dictionary in print and electronic form. Parents and caregivers also began training in sign language and Braille..FY 2022 in basic education-earmarked funding will support the extension of the activity for approximately 15 months and will address the shortage of special education teachers, highlighted because of the rapid increase in enrollment of children with disabilities in project sites. USAID will increase the capacity of general education teachers to teach children with disabilities, strengthen local capacity to develop more special education teachers, and increase parents&apos;/caregivers&apos; capacity to support sign language and Braille reading. USAID will support the Philippine Department of Education to implement the new law for inclusive education (Republic Act 11650), which requires learners with special needs to be identified and provided with free basic and quality education. . .. .</narrative>
  </description>
